/-!
# Regular Hilbert coefficient data for arbitrary defining ideals

Source: ILO VIII 2.3.11--2.3.13 (resolution route); Bierstone--Milman,
Invent. Math. 128 (1997), Chapter III, Theorems 7.14, 7.20 and 9.6;
Bierstone--Milman--Temkin, arXiv:0905.3580v1, sections 6.1--6.3 and 6.5--6.6
(blueprint: lem-per-nonreduced-regular-hilbert-coefficients).

The defining ideal is retained with its nilpotents. Vertices precede the choice of Q;
the datum itself supplies one common chart, all five conditions on its coefficient
order locus, and the pointwise bound on every extended Taylor-quotient dimension.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.HilbertCoefficient

/-- Regular Hilbert coefficient data exist without a reducedness assumption.
The zero-Taylor-ideal case has an empty vertex list, the whole chart as coefficient
support, and zero coefficient ideal. The coefficient ideal below is generated by
all coefficients whose degree is below that of their vertex; when the list is
empty this is the empty generating family.

Source: ILO VIII 2.3.11--2.3.13; BM 1997, Chapter III, (7.1)--(7.2),
Theorems 7.14, 7.20 and 9.6, pp.261--282; BMT arXiv:0905.3580v1, sections 6.1--6.6
(blueprint: lem-per-nonreduced-regular-hilbert-coefficients).

Proof:
1. Take the kernel ideal of the closed immersion, not its radical. The smooth
finite-type ambient scheme is Noetherian since its base is a field. Its ideal
is quasi-coherent and finitely generated on affine charts, hence coherent.
2. Apply lem-trf-generic-monotone-diagram to obtain regular vertex generators
and a monotone Taylor diagram. Since a belongs to X, the Taylor ideal has no
unit, so every vertex degree is positive. Separate essential transverse variables
from complementary variables and order the vertices in degree-lexicographic order.
Fix Q only after this finite vertex family is fixed.
3. Induct over degree blocks using lem-trf-block-straightening-projection-transport.
Cut out the smooth coefficient support by vertex derivatives and invert the block
Jacobian. Transport the earlier coefficient-test projection through straightening.
Apply lem-trf-exact-block-coefficient-projection: its full correction columns and
invertible finite matrix kill the new forbidden coefficients through Q, preserving
the actual ideal, the first exponents, all earlier equations and their Jacobians.
There are finitely many blocks and determinants, so one neighbourhood suffices.
4. The distinguished point lies in the coefficient order locus, because the
first exponents are the specified vertices. Apply
lem-trf-coefficient-locus-uniform-division to obtain orders equal to the vertex
degrees and unique supported homogeneous division for every degree prefix and
homogeneous degree, throughout that locus on one neighbourhood.
5. At the distinguished point, lem-trf-jet-diagram-hilbert-control computes all
Taylor-quotient dimensions from the original diagram. Straightening preserves
powers of the formal maximal ideal, and generator replacement preserves the ideal.
The dimension equality and lem-trf-homogeneous-continuous-formal-division force
the supported remainder of every ideal element to vanish. This gives supported
generation at the distinguished point and retains its initial diagram.
6. Apply lem-trf-coefficient-order-hilbert-equality with those center properties
and the derivative equations and Jacobians already valid on the coefficient support.
After shrinking, its dimension equality holds on the coefficient order locus.
At each such point, homogeneous continuous formal division again forces every
ideal-element remainder to vanish. Thus supported generation holds throughout it.
7. Apply lem-trf-extended-hilbert-local-upper-bound to the same defining ideal.
Its finite-value and finite-module realization gives one open neighbourhood on
which every Taylor-quotient dimension is at most its value at the center. Restrict
the previous data to this neighbourhood, retaining all relative Taylor variables
also at nonclosed points.
8. If the Taylor ideal is zero, the generic monotone diagram gives an empty
regular generating family after shrinking. Take the whole chart as coefficient
support and no transverse variables or coefficients. Its inclusion is an
isomorphism and the empty coefficient family generates the zero ideal. Division
has only the input remainder, generation concerns only zero, and all Jacobian
and forbidden-derivative requirements are vacuous. This also covers ambient
dimension zero. The assembled objects are the recorded regular coefficient datum.
-/
theorem exists_nonreduced_regularHilbertCoefficientDatum
    (k : Type u) [Field k] [CharZero k] (X M : Scheme.{u})
    (p : M ⟶ Spec (CommRingCat.of k)) (i : X ⟶ M) [IsClosedImmersion i]
    [Smooth p] [LocallyOfFiniteType p] [QuasiCompact p] (a : X) :
    ∃ (m n s : ℕ) (α : Fin s → Fin n →₀ ℕ),
      orderedVertices α ∧ (∀ l : Fin s, 0 < (α l).degree) ∧
      ∀ Q : ℕ, (∀ l : Fin s, (α l).degree - 1 ≤ Q) →
        ∃ D : RegularHilbertCoefficientDatum p i a m n s α Q,
          taylorIdeal (RingHom.ker (pullback.snd i D.e).appTop.hom)
              (D.taylorU D.center) = ⊥ →
            s = 0 ∧ IsIso D.j ∧
              Ideal.span {c | ∃ (l : Fin s) (γ : Fin n →₀ ℕ),
                γ.degree < (α l).degree ∧ c = D.coeff l γ} = ⊥ := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.HilbertCoefficient
